      "request_prompt_text": [
        "You are extracting image information for a document knowledge retrieval system. Your priority is faithful information preservation.\n\n1. Preserve readable text as written, including names, technical terms, identifiers, numbers, dates, percentages, units, symbols and labels.\n2. For charts and tables, extract visible titles, axis names and units, legend labels, categories and their corresponding values, and table rows and columns. Keep each value associated with its correct category. Never replace readable separate values with an approximate average or vague summary.\n3. For diagrams, preserve visible text labels and explicit relationships, connections, arrows and layer counts before briefly describing non-text visual content.\n4. For photographs or illustrations without relevant text or quantitative data, provide a concise factual description. Omit empty sections and tables.\n5. Do not invent missing text, labels, values, units or relationships. Mark unclear information as unreadable or uncertain. Never fill missing image data from a user question or expected answer.\n6. Write concise Markdown in Chinese (Simplified), preserving original labels, proper names, dataset names and technical identifiers without translation unless explicitly requested. Record useful observable information before any brief visual summary; avoid general explanations."
      ],
      "request_prompt_text_sha256": [
        "4cebb0fb92995112b9ec7e1acdd223ec39c657f7ee574ef2c623e60fb3324210"
      ],
      "finish_reason": "stop",
      "ledger_status": "SETTLED",
      "caption": "8\nPEW RESEARCH CENTER\n\nOther important findings\n\n**Voters pessimistic on how Trump will impact race relations.** Nearly half of voters (46%) say Trump’s election will lead to worse race relations, while only about half as many (25%) expect race relations to improve; 26% say his election won’t make a difference. Among Clinton voters, 84% expect race relations to worsen under Trump. Among Trump supporters, half expect improvement, while 38% say his election won’t make a difference.\n\n**Post-victory, most Trump backers confident in an accurate vote count.** In August, just 38% of registered voters who supported Trump were very confident that their vote would be counted accurately. But in the aftermath of Trump’s victory, 75% expressed confidence that their votes were counted accurately. The views of Clinton supporters showed no change: After the election 67% were confident that their votes were counted accurately.\n\n**Most expect woman president, eventually.** Following Clinton’s defeat, a sizable majority of voters (79%) still expect there will be a female president “in their lifetime.” There are no significant differences in these opinions among men and women, or Clinton supporters and Trump backers.\n\n**Voters say press has too much influence.** Voters grade the press very negatively, and most (57%) say it had too much influence on the outcome of the election. Just 27% say the press had the right amount of influence on the election, while 13% say it had too little influence. About six-in-ten Trump voters (62%) say the press had too much influence, as do 50% of Clinton voters.\n\n**图表：Will the country elect a female president in your lifetime?**\n*% of voters who say ...*\n\n| 类别 | No | Yes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| All voters | 17 | 79 |\n| Men | 16 | 81 |\n| Women | 18 | 78 |\n| Trump voters | 17 | 78 |\n| Clinton voters | 17 | 81 |\n\nNote: Don’t know responses not shown. Q46bb.\nSource: Voter callback survey conducted Nov. 10-14, 2016.\nPEW RESEARCH CENTER\n\nwww.pewresearch.org",
